In a [large randomized study of 2,149 smokers](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29248897/), graphic warnings increased negative affect, reactance, and intentions to quit, with negative emotion operating as an important pathway of influence. The unpleasantness is therefore not merely an accidental leak in an otherwise neutral information system. It is partly **what the system is designed to do**. That fact does not by itself make the method unethical, but it prevents us from describing the intervention as if it were only the transmission of facts.

Fear can be useful information, and a forceful warning can be justified, especially when the threat comes with a credible path to action. The ethical problem begins when effectiveness becomes an all-purpose moral alibi. Shouting can change a child’s behaviour, humiliation can make an employee compliant, and fear of punishment can make citizens obey rules, but contemporary ethics does not end the analysis with “well, it worked.” It asks **what else the method did to the person**.

## Punitive humanism

This is where the idea of **punitive humanism** enters. It is not ordinary punishment, because nobody says, “we want you to suffer.” The proposition is more elegant: “we will make this unpleasant for you because we want what is best for you.” The benevolent intention is precisely what makes the exercise of power harder to see.

If the person changes behaviour, the system can say the intervention worked; if the person feels fear or disgust, the system can reply that these emotions were part of the mechanism. If they avert their eyes, the warning has captured attention; if they become angry, that can be described as reactance; if they become embarrassed to show the pack, the declining social acceptability of smoking can be treated as a public-health gain. The danger is not that every one of these interpretations must be wrong, but that together they can create a framework in which almost any human response confirms the wisdom of the intervention.

A system that is difficult to catch being wrong should make itself slightly nervous. If a negative outcome cannot be recognized as negative, the method is no longer being tested, it is being defended; if a side effect is automatically renamed as another part of successful influence, the possibility of correction disappears. Serious ethics therefore has to look beyond the desired behaviour and examine the afterstate of the encounter: what happened to attention, understanding, self-regard, perceived possibility, social relations, and bodily interpretation?

The MET[Ȧ]CADEMY OF HUMANITY Manifesto adopts a related discipline: a negative result remains a result, contradiction should not be cosmetically erased, and knowledge should retain provenance, method, limits, and unknowns. That gives us room for an adult conclusion in which pictorial warnings can reduce some forms of harm while also producing psychological or social effects that deserve scrutiny. A good objective does not automatically sanctify every method, and criticism of a method does not require denial of the objective.

## When the person starts hiding

One experiment makes the problem almost physically visible. In a [randomized study of 357 smokers](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35653155/), graphic warnings increased the proportion of people who hid the pack from others at least some of the time from roughly 41 percent to 57 percent; after participants returned to ordinary packs, that behaviour moved back toward baseline. The researchers did not find a corresponding difference between groups in smoking prevalence or cigarette consumption at later follow-up.

That result is fascinating because the person did not necessarily become healthier, but became more likely to **hide the object and its public visibility**. One can reasonably argue that this is beneficial because smoking becomes less socially attractive, yet another question remains available: are we optimizing health, shame, or some mixture of the two, and should we treat them as interchangeable? The same external behaviour can arise from very different internal processes.

Research on smoking stigma is correspondingly complicated. In one experiment, a [stigmatizing anti-smoking message](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28398092/) reduced quitting intentions among participants with lower income and lower self-efficacy, with shame mediating the effect. What functions as a push for one person can therefore become another piece of evidence for helplessness in someone else. The absence of a universal effect does not make the difference irrelevant; it makes uniform communication more ethically interesting.

None of this means that all social disapproval is violence or that society must pretend dangerous behaviour is neutral. The problem begins when a description of behaviour quietly becomes a description of the person: “this behaviour carries serious risk” slides toward “this person is irresponsible,” dependence becomes weakness, and a failed quit attempt becomes proof of defective will. At that point the medical message is no longer speaking only about lungs; it is helping to manufacture a social identity.

## New saints and new sinners

Human beings have always enjoyed sorting people into morally legible categories. The costumes, flags, vocabularies, and interfaces change, but the appetite for classification survives empires with roughly the resilience of cockroaches surviving a renovation. Older societies had the righteous and the sinful, the pure and the impure, the faithful and the outsider; secular society rightly retired many of those categories, then discovered that “responsible citizen,” “unconscious consumer,” and “someone who does this to themselves” can perform surprisingly familiar work.

Nobody needs to write into law that a smoker is morally inferior. It is enough for one model of life to be repeatedly associated with sport, clean air, discipline, tasteful kitchens, and psychological maturity. Another can be paired with tumors, gangrene, dirt, addiction, and the phrase “their own fault.” What appears is a **moral inequality of interfaces**: one product is sold through freedom, status, and beauty, while another is required to carry an image of its user’s possible corpse.

That is not yet proof that the second approach is wrong, but it is proof that we are dealing with more than neutral factual transmission. A social frame is operating, and frames have consequences; somewhere between medical risk and the representation of risk sits a moral classifier deciding which behaviours warrant compulsory emotional exposure. The faithful and the unfaithful have not vanished completely. They have acquired health metrics and better typography.

## A humane prison and a frightening pack

This is why it is illuminating to place tobacco communication beside a field in which the state literally does punish people. The Norwegian Correctional Service states a [principle of normality](https://www.kriminalomsorgen.no/en/about-the-norwegian-correctional-service): the punishment is the restriction of liberty itself, other rights are not automatically removed, life inside should resemble life outside as far as possible, and conditions should not be more restrictive than security requires. That is a significant civilizational shift in the logic of punishment.

Even when a person has committed an offence, the state is learning to distinguish necessary restriction from gratuitous humiliation. Then the same modern culture can walk out of that mature room, enter a shop, and tell another adult buying a legal product to look repeatedly at a diseased organ for their own good. The comparison does not imply that a health warning is legally or psychologically equivalent to imprisonment; it reveals something subtler, namely that **different parts of humanism evolve at different speeds**.

In one room the twenty-first century already says, “do not add suffering merely because you have power,” while in another we can still treat psychological discomfort as a desirable feature when the outcome statistic is favourable. No particular country needs to be indicted for hypocrisy to notice the historical unevenness. In one institutional field humanism has learned to separate restraint from humiliation, while in another the Middle Ages has acquired Photoshop, a colour printer, and a public-health grant.

## The tobacco industry is not the innocent martyr here

There is an obvious trap at this point: criticism of regulatory design can accidentally turn into public relations for the tobacco industry. It should not. The [World Health Organization has documented](https://iris.who.int/bitstream/handle/10665/381783/WHO-EURO-2025-3700-43459-80175-eng.pdf?sequence=1) systematic industry resistance to large pictorial warnings and plain packaging, including lobbying, delay, litigation, and the use of third parties. Tobacco companies have a direct commercial interest in preserving the attractiveness and sale of the product.

Public health has a different and socially legitimate interest in reducing dependence, disease, and premature death, so the two positions are not morally symmetrical. Yet another logical shortcut remains dangerous: if one actor has a serious conflict of interest, every intervention by its opponent does not automatically become ethically optimal. A bad actor does not turn every move on the other side into a masterpiece of human-centred design.
